# Line endings. See GitHub issue #9.
#
# THE CONVENTION IS LF, IN THE REPOSITORY AND IN THE WORKING TREE.
#
# Chosen deliberately rather than by freezing what was on disk. The claim in
# CLAUDE.md that "lib/ sources are CRLF; src/ and README are LF" was only half
# true - measured before writing this, lib/ was 19 CRLF against 16 LF, mixed
# within itself, so the CRLF really was historical accident and not a
# convention worth preserving. Everywhere else was already overwhelmingly LF
# (test/ 28-0, tools/ 14-0, src/ 13-1), so LF is both the standard choice for a
# cross-platform C++ project and the one that moves the fewest files.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than a bare `text=auto` on purpose: it pins the WORKING TREE
# to LF as well, so a checked-out file is byte-identical to its blob. That is
# what actually stops the churn the issue describes - PlatformIO and the IDE
# rewriting files with nothing but line-ending changes - because after this
# there is no representation for them to rewrite it INTO. A bare `text=auto`
# would leave Windows checkouts as CRLF and keep that door open.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts break outright if they reach a shell with CRLF, so this one is
# not a style preference. scripts/release.sh is the OTA publish path.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Windows batch is the opposite case: cmd.exe wants CRLF. None are tracked
# today (publish.cmd is gitignored) but the rule belongs here before one is.
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Binary - never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png           binary
*.zip           binary
*.bin           binary

# CAD exports are stored EXACTLY as the tool wrote them, and are the one place
# this file deliberately does nothing.
#
# STEP is ASCII (AP214), so `text=auto` would happily rewrite it - and the
# three tracked files disagree with each other about line endings already:
# cad/EnclosureOnly.step is stored LF, cad/Enclosure.step and cad/Keycaps.step
# are stored CRLF. Normalising them is a 191,405-line diff across two files
# that nobody will ever read a diff of, to fix a problem they do not have:
# issue #9 is about PlatformIO and the IDE rewriting SOURCE, and neither
# touches cad/. `binary` would be wrong for the opposite reason - it freezes
# the working-tree copy rather than the stored one, which silently rewrites
# EnclosureOnly.step's blob 303900 -> 310894 bytes.
#
# So: no conversion in either direction, and whatever a CAD tool emits next is
# what gets committed. If these ever want normalising it should be its own
# commit, on purpose, not a side effect of a line-endings policy.
cad/**          -text

# Fabrication output must stay byte-stable. The README tells people to order
# the board from kicad/LVGL/jlcpcb/production_files/, so these files are
# committed deliberately (see .gitignore); Gerber and drill files are
# technically ASCII, but nothing good comes of git rewriting the bytes of
# something a board house will read.
kicad/LVGL/gerbers/**   -text
kicad/LVGL/production/**  -text
*.gbr           -text
*.gbrjob        -text
*.drl           -text
*.gtl           -text
*.gto           -text
*.gtp           -text
*.gts           -text
*.gbs           -text
*.gm1           -text
*.ipc           -text

# KiCad's own project files are text and diff usefully, but they are written by
# the tool rather than by hand - let them normalise like everything else and do
# not hand-edit them to fix a line ending.
